.TH SAFER 1
.SH NAME
safer \- run project tools in containers using proxy scripts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B safer
[OPTIONS] <CMD> <IMAGE> [DIR...]
.br
.B safer
[-d SAFER_DIR]
.br
.B safer
-i TOOL
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B safer
creates a CMD script to run a dev tool in the current directory using a
container IMAGE.
.P
The current directory, and any extra DIR specified, are mounted in the
container using the same paths.
.P
The scripts are created in the SAFER_DIR directory,
.B .safer
by default. You can run
.B safer
without any option to get a new definition for the
.B PATH
environment variable. You can also run
.B 'eval $(safer)'
to make the scripts available immediately.
.P
When used with
.B -i
,
.B safer
installs a Containerfile for the TOOL from its internal library.
.P
See
.B NOTES
for more details.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-e " entrypoint"
Tool entrypoint in the image.
.TP
.BR \-p " port[:port]"
Port to expose.
.TP
.BR \-k
Run the tool in a persistent container.
.TP
.BR \-K " command"
Command used to persist the container (implies -k).
.br
Default: sleep inf
.TP
.BR \-a " argument"
Argument injected to stop persisting containers (implies -k).
.br
Default: --safer-stop
.TP
.BR \-n " name"
Use this name to persist the container (implies -k).
.br
Takes precedence over -N.
.TP
.BR \-N " name"
Similar to -n, but the CWD will be added, as plain -k does.
.br
Will not be used if -n is specified.
.TP
.BR \-d " directory"
Directory for the scripts.
.br
Default: .safer
.TP
.BR \-R " (podman|docker)"
Runtime CLI command. Overrides podman/docker detection.
.TP
.BR \-X " args"
Runtime extra args.
.br
Docker default: --user "$(id -u):$(id -g)"
.TP
.BR \-f
Force script replacement.
.TP
.BR \-t " (AUTO|BOTH|STDIN|TTY|NONE)"
Override stdin/pseudo-tty defaults.
.TP
.BR \-i " tool"
Install a Containerfile from the library.
.TP
.BR \-I " tool"
Install and build a Containerfile from the library.
.br
Overrides -i .
.TP
.BR \-v
Display the current version.
.TP
.BR \-h
Display usage and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR SAFER_DIR
Directory for the scripts.
.br
Default: .safer
.TP
.BR SAFER_LIB_PATH
Directories for the library. See
.BR FILES.
.TP
.BR SAFER_RT_CMD
Runtime CLI command. See
.BR NOTES.
.TP
.BR SAFER_RT_ARGS
Runtime extra args. See
.BR NOTES.
.TP
.BR SAFER_PERSIST_CMD
Command used to persist the container.
.br
Default: sleep inf
.TP
.BR SAFER_PERSIST_STOP_CMD
Argument injected to stop persisting containers.
.br
Default: --safer-stop
.SH FILES
.TP
.BR /etc/safer ", "~/.saferrc ", ".saferrc
These files are sourced by safer.
.TP
.BR ~/.local/share/safer ", "/usr/share/safer ", "/usr/local/share/safer
These are the default locations for the
.B images
directory used by the -i option.
.SH NOTES
.P
PERSISTENT CONTAINERS
.P
Instead of running a new container each time the CMD is called, you can
run
.BR safer
with
.BR -k .
In this mode, a named persistent container will be created by the script
and the commands will execute using
.BR exec,
preserving the state of the container across executions. This container
can be stopped using the CMD with the injected argument
.BR --safer-stop.
.P
The container name will be the CMD with the CWD as a suffix. You can
specify a custom name with
.BR -n
or
.BR -N
if you also want the CWD suffixed to it.
.P
RUNTIME DETECTION
.P
.B safer
will try to detect a container runtime. It will prefer
.B podman
over
.B docker.
.BR SAFER_RT_CMD
and
.BR SAFER_RT_ARGS
will override this detection.
.P
If
.B docker
is selected, '--user "$(id -u):$(id -g)"' will be used as the default
runtime arguments.
.P
PSEUDO-TTY ALLOCATION
.P
To ensure interactive CMDs can be used by default, the scripts created by
.BR safer
will ask the container runtimes to pass STDIN and will try to guess
when to allocate a pseudo-tty. Some tools using the CMD
may require different combinations of these two features. Use
.BR -t " MODE"
to override what
.BR safer
scripts request by default.
.P
.BR AUTO
is the default (pass STDIN and guess the pseudo-tty).
.BR BOTH
will always enable both options.
.BR STDIN
will only pass STDIN.
.BR TTY
will only allocate a pseudo-tty. And
.BR NONE
will enable neither.
.SH CAVEATS
.P
PHYSICAL/ABSOLUTE PATHS
.P
.B safer
enforces absolute paths in scripts generation. This is consistent with
how container runtimes work but may break host tools if they need path
information from inside the container.
